# inventory.ini.example
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES:
# Tells Ansible which machine(s) to configure and how to reach them.
# This is Ansible's most fundamental concept: it's "agentless" — there's
# no Ansible daemon running on the target host. Every run just SSHes in,
# copies over a small Python payload, runs it, and cleans up. The
# inventory file is simply the address book.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS FOR LEARNING:
# Notice this is a *separate* file/tool from Terraform, even though both
# are "infrastructure" tools. That split is deliberate and is a common
# real-world pattern:
#   - Terraform/OpenTofu = provisioning ("does this server exist, with
#     this size, this network access, this disk?")
#   - Ansible = configuration management ("given a server that already
#     exists, get the right software installed and configured on it")
# Terraform is bad at the second job (its provisioners are a last resort,
# explicitly discouraged in HashiCorp's own docs) and Ansible is bad at
# the first (it has no real concept of cloud resource lifecycle/state).
# Using each for what it's good at is the standard pairing you'll see in
# real job postings that mention both tools.
#
# Copy this to inventory.ini and fill in the real IP — or better, generate
# it automatically (see the README "Run it" section) using:
#   echo "[k3s_node]
#   $(tofu output -raw public_ip) ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/jenops_key_pair.pem" > inventory.ini

[k3s_node]
REPLACE_WITH_PUBLIC_IP ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/jenops_key_pair.pem

[k3s_node:vars]
ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
# Ubuntu AMIs prompt to accept SSH host keys on first connect; this is
# fine for a personal test lab. In a real team environment you'd manage
# known_hosts properly instead of disabling checking.
ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new'
